---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";

// ASSUMPTION: freezing and storage are delivered through an embryology laboratory
// (in-clinic or partner). Confirm with the clinic before publishing.

const page = {
  slug: "fertility-preservation",
  name: "Egg & Sperm Freezing",
  title: "Egg & Sperm Freezing in Lahore | Fertility Preservation",
  description:
    "Egg freezing and sperm freezing in Lahore: who it suits, how vitrification works, the process, success factors, risks and costs. Fertility preservation guidance.",
  eyebrow: "Fertility Preservation",
  h1: "Egg and Sperm Freezing in Lahore",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to ask about egg or sperm freezing (fertility preservation) with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book a fertility preservation consultation",
  heroSub: [
    "Fertility preservation means freezing your own eggs or sperm now so they can be used later to try for a pregnancy. By cryopreserving gametes, the aging of those cells is effectively paused even as your body continues to age.",
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic in Lahore, Dr. Kamran Saleem advises patients (men and women) on egg and sperm freezing, whether it’s urgent (for example, before cancer treatment) or elective (for family planning). He will explain clearly what freezing can — and cannot — guarantee.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    "If you need fertility preservation before cancer treatment or another procedure that threatens fertility, please contact us as early as possible. Treatment can often be started quickly, even on short notice, so timing is critical.",
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "what-is-fertility-preservation",
      title: "What Is Fertility Preservation?",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        { rich: ["Fertility preservation is the process of saving or protecting eggs, sperm, or reproductive tissue for use in having children in the future. In practice, it means collecting eggs or sperm today and freezing them in liquid nitrogen at very low temperatures. These frozen gametes can remain stored for many years. When you are ready, the cells are thawed and used in an assisted reproduction cycle – typically ", ["in vitro fertilization (IVF)", `${T}/ivf`], ". Eggs are thawed, fertilized by ", ["intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI)", `${T}/icsi`], ", and resulting embryos can be transferred back into the uterus. Likewise, frozen sperm can be thawed for use in IVF or intrauterine insemination."] },
        "Egg freezing uses vitrification – an ultra-rapid freezing technique – to prevent ice crystals from forming in the cells. In vitrification, eggs (and embryos) are plunged almost instantly into liquid nitrogen (−196 °C), turning their contents into a glass-like solid. This “flash” cryopreservation gives much higher survival than older slow-freeze methods. Sperm cryopreservation is simpler: a semen sample is mixed with a cryoprotectant solution and frozen in a cryobank, also in liquid nitrogen. Overall, freezing allows you to “stop the clock” on your gametes’ biological age – but it does not change the age or health of your own body or uterus.",
        { rich: ["Eggs and sperm can potentially remain frozen indefinitely. In practice, there is “no known expiration date” on frozen eggs and human sperm has even been stored successfully for over 20 years. When you decide to conceive, your fertility team will thaw the frozen cells and complete the usual ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " steps (", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], " and embryo transfer for eggs; IUI or IVF for sperm)."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "who-should-consider-freezing",
      title: "Who Should Consider Egg or Sperm Freezing?",
      icon: "user",
      body: [
        "Fertility freezing is especially urgent for anyone about to undergo medical treatments that can damage fertility. This includes cancer therapies (chemotherapy, radiation) or surgeries that affect the ovaries or testes. For example, women facing chemotherapy for leukemia or men with testicular cancer should consider egg or sperm banking before treatment begins. The American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO) and other experts list cancer patients as prime candidates for fertility preservation.",
        "Beyond cancer, other situations include:",
        { h3: "Women" },
        { ul: [
          "Severe or recurrent endometriosis (which may reduce ovarian reserve), autoimmune diseases requiring toxic treatments, a family history of early menopause, or simply wanting to delay childbearing for personal, career or educational reasons. Many healthy women freeze eggs electively to “pause their clock” and improve future fertility odds when they are older.",
        ] },
        { h3: "Men" },
        { ul: [
          "Declining sperm counts or motility, planned vasectomy (should future childbearing be reconsidered), surgical removal of a testicle, or undergoing high-risk work or military service are all reasons to bank sperm. In practice, any man about to have chemotherapy, pelvic surgery or testicular radiation should freeze sperm first. Men may also freeze sperm as a backup before an IVF cycle or ICSI (for example, if sperm is retrieved during a procedure).",
        ] },
        "In short, fertility preservation is for anyone who faces medical treatments or conditions that threaten fertility, as well as for those who wish to safeguard future parenthood. If your health team recommends gonadotoxic treatment or if you simply want insurance against age-related decline, Dr. Kamran can discuss whether freezing is right for you.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "egg-freezing-process",
      title: "The Egg Freezing Process",
      icon: "calendar",
      body: [
        { rich: ["Egg freezing uses the same first steps as an ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " cycle. A typical cycle takes about two to three weeks from start to finish. The main stages are:"] },
        { h3: "1. Assessment (Consultation and Testing)" },
        "You meet with a fertility specialist for a detailed consultation. Blood tests (including AMH) and a vaginal ultrasound count your ovarian follicles to estimate your ovarian reserve. This helps predict how many eggs you might yield. (We will also perform routine health checks and ensure you’re fit for treatment.)",
        { h3: "2. Ovarian Stimulation (10–14 days)" },
        "You give yourself daily hormone injections (usually FSH and LH analogs) for about 10–12 days. These medications stimulate multiple ovarian follicles to mature simultaneously, rather than the single follicle of a normal cycle. We monitor your ovaries closely with blood tests and ultrasounds every few days, adjusting doses as needed.",
        { h3: "3. Egg Retrieval (Outpatient Procedure)" },
        "When the eggs are mature, Dr. Kamran will perform the egg collection under light sedation. Using ultrasound guidance, a needle is passed through the vaginal wall into the ovaries and follicular fluid is aspirated. The procedure takes only 15–30 minutes, with no surgical incisions. Most patients go home the same day. You should not feel pain during retrieval, thanks to the sedation. Afterward, mild cramping or bloating is common for a day or two.",
        { h3: "4. Vitrification and Storage" },
        "In the lab, embryologists identify the mature eggs and immediately vitrify (freeze) them. The ultra-rapid freezing at –196 °C locks in the eggs’ state. The frozen eggs are then stored in secure cryotanks indefinitely. You will receive legal consent forms explaining storage duration, annual fees, and what happens if you decide not to use them or cannot maintain payments.",
        { h3: "5. Future Use" },
        { rich: ["When you want to use your eggs, they are thawed and fertilized with sperm by ", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], " in the IVF lab. The resulting embryos (usually 2–3 per transfer) are cultured and then transferred into the uterus (or potentially frozen again for a later transfer). The whole cycle from thaw to transfer is the same as a standard ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], "/ICSI cycle."] },
        "In urgent cases (for example, an imminent start of cancer therapy), stimulation can sometimes begin at any point in the menstrual cycle (known as random-start protocols), minimizing delays. We coordinate carefully with your oncology or surgical team to time everything.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "sperm-freezing-process",
      title: "The Sperm Freezing Process",
      icon: "bolt",
      body: [
        "Sperm freezing is much faster and simpler than egg freezing. Usually just one short visit is needed. On the day, you will abstain from ejaculation for 2–3 days beforehand to optimize semen quality. At the clinic, you provide a semen sample by masturbation. The sample is first analyzed (sperm count, motility, morphology) to assess its quality.",
        "Next, the semen is mixed with a cryoprotectant solution that shields sperm from damage during freezing. The treated sample is then loaded into storage vials and rapidly frozen in liquid nitrogen (–196 °C). The whole process can be completed in under an hour. You should produce a few separate samples (if possible) to maximize chances; more frozen vials means more options later. All samples are labeled and banked securely.",
        { rich: ["It’s important to understand that not all sperm survive the freeze-thaw process. Typically only about half of the sperm remain motile after thawing. That’s why the number of vials and the pre-freeze sperm count both matter: higher count and multiple samples improve the odds. In some men with very low or no sperm in the ejaculate (azoospermia), sperm can be retrieved directly from the testes via procedures like micro-TESE and then frozen. See our ", ["male infertility treatment", `${T}/male-infertility`], " page for details."] },
        { rich: ["Later, when you decide to attempt a pregnancy, the frozen vials are thawed and the sperm are used for fertility treatment (IUI or ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], "/", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], "). Modern sperm cryopreservation has a long track record – human sperm has been used successfully after decades of storage – but success still depends on starting quality."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "freezing-success-rates",
      title: "Success Rates and Limits",
      icon: "heart",
      body: [
        "Egg and sperm freezing offer a chance, but never a guarantee, at a future pregnancy. The ultimate success depends on many factors. The most important is age at freezing. For women, egg quality (and quantity) declines with age, especially after 35. Eggs frozen at a younger age are much more likely to fertilize and develop into healthy embryos. In general, clinicians often recommend banking 15–30 eggs to give a good chance of at least one live birth later. Multiple stimulation cycles may be needed to reach a sufficient egg bank.",
        "For men, fertility also decreases gradually with age, but most men remain fertile longer than women. Sperm freezing is straightforward and works well provided there are enough healthy sperm. Regardless, no freezing method can remove all age-related risks. For example, freezing eggs won’t prevent pregnancy complications associated with carrying a pregnancy in a woman’s 40s.",
        "In summary, “old eggs frozen young” have success rates comparable to fresh eggs of that younger age, whereas freezing at older ages yields lower success. Both Northernwestern Medicine and Cleveland Clinic note that success varies by age, initial cell number, and clinic expertise. Even at their best, preservation methods only offer a chance at future fertility, not certainty.",
        "Your consultation will include an honest estimate of your individual odds, based on your ovarian reserve (or sperm count) and age. We recommend realistic planning: freezing for insurance, but also discussing other family-building options (egg/sperm donation, adoption, etc.) if needed later.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "freezing-risks",
      title: "Risks and Considerations",
      icon: "shield",
      body: [
        "Fertility preservation is safe for most people but is a medical procedure with some risks and implications:",
        { ul: [
          "Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS) – When stimulating the ovaries, there is a rare risk of OHSS, where the ovaries swell and leak fluid. Modern protocols and monitoring make OHSS uncommon, but we watch closely for symptoms during stimulation.",
          "Egg retrieval procedure – Although minor, the sedation and vaginal needle puncture carry small risks of bleeding, infection, or injury. These complications are very rare in experienced hands. After retrieval you may have mild cramping or spotting.",
          "Emotional and financial strain – Undergoing fertility treatment can be stressful, especially if multiple cycles are needed. The process involves daily shots and frequent clinic visits, which can be emotionally and physically taxing. The financial cost is also significant (see below). It’s important to have support and realistic expectations.",
          "Unused gametes – In many cases, once patients conceive naturally or with frozen gametes, leftover eggs or sperm remain unused. This is not harmful, but it means many frozen gametes may never result in a baby – which is a good outcome if you succeed naturally. We encourage you to discuss in advance what you would like done with any unused gametes (discard, donate for research, etc.) as part of your consent.",
        ] },
        "According to expert sources, the main benefit of fertility preservation is the chance to have a biological child in the future despite current circumstances. The main risks are those of the procedures above, plus the emotional and financial costs. Dr. Kamran will review all potential side effects (like OHSS, sedation risks, etc.) and make sure you feel comfortable proceeding.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "storage-and-consent",
      title: "Storage, Consent and Cost",
      icon: "document",
      body: [
        "Fertility preservation involves long-term storage of your genetic material, so the financial and legal terms must be clear from the start. In your consent discussion we will cover storage duration, annual storage fees, ownership and decision rights, and what happens if you can’t pay fees or your situation changes (e.g. divorce, death, etc.). All of this will be documented in written consent forms in accordance with medical ethics and local regulations.",
        { rich: ["Cost structure: Egg freezing costs are higher than sperm freezing because of the complexity of ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " procedures. As a guideline: the total cost of an egg-freezing cycle covers the consultation, ovarian reserve tests, ~10–14 days of stimulation medication, ultrasound/blood monitoring, the egg retrieval procedure, embryology lab fees for vitrification, and then annual storage. (Later use of the eggs will require a separate IVF/ICSI cycle cost.) Likewise, sperm freezing costs include the initial semen analysis, the cryopreservation process, and yearly storage charges. According to one Pakistani fertility center, a full IVF cycle is on the order of ₨830,000–970,000 (Pakistani rupees); sperm banking may be included in such packages or may add a smaller fee. Actual prices vary by clinic and medicines used. Dr. Kamran will provide an itemized estimate for your plan during consultation. For a full breakdown, read our guide to ", ["IVF treatment in Lahore: cost, process and tests", "/resources/ivf-treatment-in-lahore"], "."] },
        "Regardless of costs, fertility preservation is often seen as a form of “insurance.” Even though many eggs/sperm might never be needed, spending for the chance to have a child later is priceless to some patients. We will also discuss any possible support, insurance or employer benefits that could help cover part of the cost.",
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "What is egg freezing?", a: "Egg freezing (oocyte cryopreservation) is a process where a woman’s mature eggs are collected after ovarian stimulation and then frozen for future use. The eggs are vitrified (rapidly frozen) and stored in liquid nitrogen. Later, when pregnancy is desired, the eggs are thawed and fertilized by ICSI, and resulting embryos are transferred to the uterus." },
    { q: "What is the best age for egg freezing?", a: "Younger is better. Fertility and egg quality decline with age, so women in their 20s or early 30s generally have higher success rates from frozen eggs than those who freeze later. Most experts recommend freezing eggs before age 35 if possible. However, the right timing also depends on your personal health and family plans." },
    { q: "Does egg freezing guarantee a baby?", a: "No. Freezing eggs gives you a chance at pregnancy, but it cannot guarantee a baby. Success depends on your age at freezing, how many mature eggs you store, and factors during the IVF cycle (thaw survival, embryo quality, etc.). Some patients use multiple cycles to bank enough eggs. Even with younger eggs, there is always some chance that none will lead to a pregnancy." },
    { q: "Is egg retrieval painful?", a: "No, egg retrieval is done under sedation (light anesthesia), so you should not feel pain during the procedure. It is a short outpatient needle aspiration guided by ultrasound. Afterward, most women experience only mild cramping or bloating for a day or two. Serious complications are very rare." },
    { q: "How long can eggs and sperm be stored?", a: "Indefinitely, as long as fees are paid. There is no known expiration date for frozen eggs or sperm. Human fertility clinics have successfully used frozen sperm after decades of storage. In practice, clinics set rules on maximum storage time (often many years), but many patients keep their gametes frozen for 10+ years." },
    { q: "Can men freeze sperm before cancer treatment?", a: "Yes. Sperm freezing is quick and can usually be arranged immediately before treatment starts. Men about to undergo chemotherapy, radiation or surgery affecting the testes are strongly advised to bank sperm first. Several samples are often collected if time allows, to maximize future fertility options." },
    { q: "How soon can freezing be done before cancer treatment?", a: "As soon as possible. In many cases we can begin ovarian stimulation at any point in the menstrual cycle, so egg freezing can often be completed within a couple of weeks. Sperm freezing takes only a day or two. It is best to contact us immediately after a cancer diagnosis so we can coordinate with your oncologist and start the preservation cycle without delay." },
    { q: "What are the costs?", a: "Costs vary. Egg freezing includes the cost of tests, medications, the egg retrieval procedure, lab freezing, and annual storage fees. Sperm freezing is less expensive (analysis, cryopreservation and storage). For reference, a single IVF cycle in Pakistan currently runs around ₨830,000–970,000, so egg freezing (an IVF half-cycle) is on that scale, while sperm freezing adds a modest fee. You will receive an exact quote during your consultation based on your individual plan." },
    { q: "Will I definitely use the frozen eggs/sperm?", a: "Many patients end up conceiving naturally or using some of the frozen gametes, but it’s possible to never use all the stored material. This is usually considered a success (if you had a baby without needing them!). We encourage you to plan ahead: in your consent, specify what should happen to any unused eggs or sperm (options include continuing storage, donation to research, or disposal) once your family is complete." },
    { q: "Do fertility clinics in Lahore have the equipment for vitrification?", a: "Yes. Clinics use modern cryopreservation equipment and protocols. For example, one Lahore center reports using flash-vitrification (immersion in liquid nitrogen at –196 °C) with over 98% post-thaw survival of eggs and embryos. Dr. Kamran works with certified embryology labs to ensure your eggs or sperm are frozen with the highest survival rates possible." },
    { q: "What factors affect the success of fertility preservation?", a: "Key factors include age at freezing, your ovarian reserve or sperm count, and the number of eggs/sperm banked. Clinic expertise and lab quality also matter. Younger patients with many eggs or high sperm count naturally have better odds. Dr. Kamran will review these factors with you to give a personalized expectation." },
    { q: "How can I book an appointment?", a: "Please contact Kamran Fertility Clinic by phone or WhatsApp (☎ +92 314-8432789). Let us know you are interested in fertility preservation. We will schedule a consultation to answer all your questions and get any needed tests (like AMH) started quickly. Time is important, so we strive to see urgent cases at the earliest possible slot." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Fertility Preservation in Lahore With Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  closingParas: [
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic, we offer personalized, evidence-based guidance on egg and sperm freezing in Lahore. Dr. Kamran Saleem (MBBS, PGCert UK) will explain the realistic benefits and limitations of freezing in your case. We coordinate the ovarian stimulation and sperm collection in our clinic, and partner with state-of-the-art embryology labs for vitrification and long-term storage. Whether you need urgent preservation before medical treatment or are planning ahead for family building, our team is ready to help you understand every step.",
    "Book a consultation today to discuss fertility preservation options. Early planning gives you the best chance. Send us a message or call our clinic so we can arrange the earliest possible appointment and start preserving your future fertility now.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/ivf-treatment-in-lahore", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "IVF Treatment in Lahore: Cost, Process, Tests & Choosing the Right Clinic" },
    { href: "/resources/male-infertility--treatment", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "Male Infertility Treatment: Causes, Tests and Options" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Thinking About Preserving Your Fertility?",
    body: "If time is short because of medical treatment, message us first so we can arrange the earliest possible appointment.",
    cta: "Book a Fertility Preservation Consultation",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
